  "body": "Stacked on #2968. Review the [die opening changes above the parent](https://github.com/qsmits/Camcraft/compare/6201b325cd26be81c9938e7b485124af42864ccf...a6e3a1310cae5f94df32b930c7e4a44da8b54845).\n\n**Safety review pending on `a6e3a1310`.** This branch predates the zero-step writer in #2951. The control results below describe the die opening on the tested installation. Before merge or machine use, this branch must adopt the #2984 correction and verify that the control imports the work with zero erosion steps.\n\nSelect the top rim of a modeled through-opening on a retained plate. Check the complete top and bottom cap boundaries and all vertical walls. Match the selected rim to the actual void. Require an interior threading hole and a clear lead. Emit the nominal boundary with the native die cut side. The resident control applies the offset. The first supported die process uses 20 mm stock.\n\nRun the synthetic plate from the repository root with `cargo run -p camcraft-app --bin camcraft-cli -- script examples/agie/die-plate.camscript`. The operation generated 41 moves without a warning. The nominal 38-sided opening has straight sides and faceted semicircular ends. CAMCraft reported 760.19 square millimetres of captured waste. The runnable example, guide and tests include the geometry selection.\n\nAgie Control plotted all 38 faces at the four inward offsets of 0.245, 0.155, 0.140 and 0.134 mm. It cut the main profile, completed a 1.000 mm rough separation at X −12.255, then ran three complete trims. The threading point stayed at (−5.5, 0). The formal check reported zero job errors. The control requested reference and machining setup and reported option 131072. No physical cut was made.\n\nThe independent gate passed all three die integration tests, eleven native Agie app tests and twelve Agie post tests. Strict Clippy passed for the app, geometry and post libraries.\n\nEffort: high\nChangelog: The native Agie post accepts an explicitly selected convex through-opening on a 20 mm plate. It checks the retained material, lead and inward rough offset before writing the job.\n\nFixes #2957\n",
